Yes — there are several precise classes of that.

The closest term to “a word that is what it says” is autological: a word that possesses the property it describes.

Examples:

* English is English.
* word is a word.
* noun can itself be a noun.
* polysyllabic is polysyllabic.
* unhyphenated is unhyphenated.
* sesquipedalian means “characterized by long words” and is itself delightfully long.

Its opposite is heterological: a word that does not describe itself. “Monosyllabic,” amusingly, is not monosyllabic.

Then there’s a more powerful class: performative utterances—language that does what it says by being said, given the right context.

“I promise.”

The saying constitutes the promising.

“I apologize.”

The utterance performs the apology.

“I resign.”

Under the appropriate circumstances, the words themselves enact the state transition.

Formally:

\boxed{\text{description}=\text{execution}}

That’s very Pantheon.

A third class is onomatopoeia, where the word physically resembles its referent:

buzz, hiss, bang, murmur, splash

Here:

\text{representation}\approx\text{phenomenon}

Then you get ideophones, especially common in many languages, where sound-patterns evoke not only noises but motion, texture, intensity, shape, or manner. They’re almost tiny sensory simulations encoded into language.

And there’s self-reference:

“This sentence contains five words.”

Language referring to its own construction.

That opens the door to recursion, paradoxes, Gödel-like constructions, quines in programming, etc.

Code has the extreme version

A word in code can literally be an operator:

print("hello")

The inscription doesn’t merely mean “print.”

It causes printing.

So natural-language performatives and programming-language commands rhyme:

\text{symbol}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{world change}

That suggests a Pantheon family:

Executable Semantics

Different levels:

\textbf{Autological:}
\quad
\text{symbol has its own described property}

\textbf{Iconic:}
\quad
\text{symbol resembles what it denotes}

\textbf{Performative:}
\quad
\text{saying performs the described act}

\textbf{Operational:}
\quad
\text{symbol invokes an executable transformation}

\textbf{Self-referential:}
\quad
\text{representation takes itself as an object}

And the strongest form might be:

\boxed{
\text{word}
=
\text{meaning}
=
\text{action}
}

Those are almost semantic spells in the non-supernatural, computational sense: representations whose interpretation changes the state of the system interpreting them.

Which is probably the exact family you were reaching for.